# Jest Testing Takeaways

- Favor unit tests for non-React modules (pure functions, CRUD helpers).
- Mock Orbit helpers (`related`, `findRecord`) and other dependencies; keep test data minimal.
- Cover edge cases: missing relationships, missing current id, filtering, wrap-around.
- Prefer deterministic sorting in tests (explicit `sequencenum`).
- Use `--runInBand --watchAll=false` to avoid hanging runs in CI/automation.

## No `require()` (`@typescript-eslint/no-require-imports`)

CI forbids CommonJS `require()` in `.ts`/`.tsx` (including specs). Use ESM
`import`. Do not add `eslint-disable` for this to make `jest.resetModules()`
work.

- Prefer **call-time** reads of `window.api` in production (`const ipc = () =>
window?.api`) so tests can `import { launch }` and stub `window.api` in
  `beforeEach` / per test.
- If a module still captures a global at load, **change that capture** rather
  than `require()`-ing after `resetModules()`.

## Custom hooks (`renderHook`)

- Use `renderHook` from `@testing-library/react` (top-level import is fine for normal
  specs). Wrap async updates in `act`.
- When a hook returns an async function, `await act(async () => { await result.current(args); })`.
- Mocks of other hooks (`useProjectDefaults`, etc.) must match the **real return shape**
  from the implementation (field names and types), or TypeScript and runtime behavior
  drift. Prefer reading the source of the hook being mocked before stubbing.

## `useGlobal` in hook tests

- Real `useGlobal` returns `[value, setter]`. Match that in mocks.
- **Keyed map pattern** (see `ConsultantCheck.test.tsx`, `useStepPermission.test.ts`):
  `mockImplementation((key: string) => { const mockValues: Record<string, unknown> = { memory, plan, project, ...overrides }; return [mockValues[key], jest.fn()]; })`.
- **Factory + `beforeEach` pattern** (see `useSectionIdDescription.test.tsx`): `jest.mock(..., () => ({ useGlobal: jest.fn() }))`, then
  `(useGlobal as jest.Mock).mockImplementation(...)` or `mockReturnValue([...])` when the hook only reads one key.
- For hooks that call `useGlobal` many times, centralize defaults and spread **overrides** once (e.g. offline / offlineOnly) so each key stays consistent.

## `useSelector` / `react-redux` in hook tests

- Mock **`react-redux`** with at least `useSelector: jest.fn()`. Many component tests also stub **`shallowEqual: jest.fn()`** (e.g. `ConsultantCheck.test.tsx`, `MediaPlayer.test.tsx`) so the mock matches what libraries import.
- **Run the real selector against a stub state** (see `burrito/useCreateBurrito.test.ts`): build a minimal object (often `Pick<IState, 'books'>` or a slice typed like `IBookNameData`) and use
  `(useSelector as unknown as jest.Mock).mockImplementation((sel: (s: IState) => unknown) => sel(mockState as IState))`.
  That way the hook’s `(state) => state.books.bookData` (or similar) is exercised and `allBookData` stays aligned with the stub.
- **TypeScript:** assigning `mockImplementation` on `useSelector` often requires **`as unknown as jest.Mock`**; a direct `as jest.Mock` cast may fail under ts-jest (`UseSelector` vs `Mock` overlap).

## `useOrbitData` and `useEffect` dependency churn

- `useOrbitData` is typically mocked with `jest.fn()` / `mockImplementation((model) => ...)`.
- If the mock returns a **new array literal on every call** (e.g. `return [{ id: 'x' }]` inline), the hook sees a **new `mediaFiles` / `sections` / `passages` reference every render**. Any `useEffect` that lists those in its dependency array will run **every render**.
- Combined with effects that call `setState` when derived data changes, that can produce **maximum update depth** errors or **heap OOM** in Jest.
- Prefer **stable references**: e.g. `let orbitMedia: MediaFileD[] = [];` set in `beforeEach`, and `if (model === 'mediafile') return orbitMedia` (reassign `orbitMedia` only when a test needs different records). Same idea for `section` / `passage` stubs.

## Test file layout (`jest.mock` vs imports)

- Put **`jest.mock(...)` calls before importing the module under test**, after any **hoist-safe** values the factory needs (e.g. `const mockMemory = { ... }` defined above the mock, as in `useStepPermission.test.ts` / `doChapter.test.ts`).
- Place **type-only imports** with the rest of imports after mocks if that keeps factories clean; Jest hoists mocks, but readability matches other specs in this repo.

## Mocking React `Context` / `useContext`

- **`jest.mock` factory + outer-scope `createContext` is fragile with ESM.** If the factory returns `{ MyContext: mockContext }` and `mockContext` is assigned in the test file **above** the mock, the **module under test** can be evaluated **before** that assignment runs (import hoisting / evaluation order). The mock then exports **`undefined`**, and `useContext(MyContext)` throws (e.g. `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading '$$typeof')`).
- **Prefer creating the context inside the mock factory** so it always exists when the mock initializes, e.g. `jest.mock('.../MyContext', () => { const React = jest.requireActual<typeof import('react')>('react'); return { MyContext: React.createContext(defaultValue) }; })`.
- **Use the same mocked export for the provider** in tests: `import { MyContext } from '.../MyContext'` (resolves to the mock) and wrap with `<MyContext.Provider value={...}>`. That guarantees `Provider` and `useContext(MyContext)` refer to **one** context object.
- **Do not** rely on a separate `const MockMyContext = React.createContext(...)` in the spec **unless** it is guaranteed to run before any import of the component that consumes the context; when in doubt, create the context **only** inside the factory (see `TeamCheckReferenceMobile.test.tsx`).

## Barrel imports and Jest parse failures

- If the hook under test imports from a **barrel** (e.g. `from '../utils'`), Jest may
  load unrelated files (`redux`, `react-localization` ESM) and fail with
  “Cannot use import statement outside a module”.
- In tests, `jest.mock('../utils', () => ({ onlyWhatYouNeed: ... }))` is often enough.
- Alternatively, change the production import to a **leaf path** (e.g.
  `from '../utils/useNum2BookCode'`) only when that is an intentional refactor, not
  only to fix tests.

## Module-level `window` / global capture

- If the module does **`const ipc = window?.api` (or similar) at top level**, that
  value is fixed when the module is first loaded. Setting `window.api` in `beforeEach`
  **after** a top-level `import` of the module under test will **not** update `ipc`.
- **Fix production** with a getter (`const ipc = () => window?.api as MainAPI | undefined`)
  so tests import the module normally and assign `window.api` before calling into it
  (see `launch.ts` / `launch.test.ts`).
- **Do not** use `jest.resetModules()` + `require()` to work around this — it fails
  `@typescript-eslint/no-require-imports`.
- **Do not** use `jest.isolateModules` for this pattern with **React hooks**: it
  loads a **second copy** of `react`, which causes “Invalid hook call” /
  `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useRef')` when `renderHook` runs the hook.

## Cutting heavy import graphs (hooks)

- If the hook imports a module that pulls **ESM-only** or **deep** trees
  (e.g. `useCompression` → localization/redux), **`jest.mock` that module** with the
  minimal runtime shape the hook needs (often a constant like `ApmDim` only; type-only
  imports are erased and need not be on the mock). This complements the barrel-import
  section above when the problematic file is a **direct** import, not only a barrel.

## Component / RTL: tables, dialogs, and timing

- **`getAllByRole('row')` on a `<table>`** may or may not include header rows depending on
  environment; **numeric row indices are brittle** for “click the *n*th data row.” Prefer:
  - scoping to **`tbody`** (e.g. `table.querySelector('tbody')` + `within(tbody)`) and then
    `getAllByRole('row')`, or
  - finding the row by **visible cell text** the user cares about (e.g. time range in the
    Limits column), or
  - `within(table).getAllByRole('rowgroup')` — note **both `thead` and `tbody` are rowgroups**,
    so do not assume “last rowgroup” is tbody without checking order.
- **Unscoped `screen.findByText` / `getByText` for segment times** (e.g. `0:00-0:10`) can
  **false-match** or pass before the **grid** has updated. Assert times **inside `tbody`**
  (or another stable container) when the value must come from the table.
- **Async setup before mocked “player” / segment callbacks:** if the real flow needs passage
  rows to exist first, add a **`waitFor`** (or similar) on a stable signal (e.g. first
  `verse-reference-*` input value) **before** `act` + `mockPlayerAction` so `handleSegment`
  runs against the same table state as in the app.
- **`getByRole('dialog' | 'heading', { name: '...' })` / accessible name:** with MUI
  `Dialog` + `DialogTitle` + `aria-labelledby`, the **computed** name in JSDOM may not always
  match a long, composed string; if a query fails, check `screen.debug()` / printed a11y
  tree, or assert via **`within(dialog)` + partial name regex** or **`#id` + `textContent`**
  for the title node when appropriate.

## Asserting props on a mocked child component

- When a component under test is mocked with `jest.fn(() => <div />)` (see
  `TeamCheckReferenceMobile.test.tsx` / `TeamCheckReference.test.tsx`), prefer
  `mock.calls[0][0]` to read the props it was called with over
  `toHaveBeenCalledWith(propsMatcher, ...)`.
- React 19 function components are invoked with a **second argument of
  `undefined`** (no legacy context), not `{}`. `toHaveBeenCalledWith(matcher, {})`
  false-fails against that `undefined`; `expect.anything()` fails too (it
  excludes `null`/`undefined`). Index into `mock.calls[N][0]` directly instead
  of asserting the full call signature.

## Give mocked components a `displayName`

- When a `jest.mock(...)` factory defines a **named function component** to
  stand in for the real one (as opposed to an inline arrow passed straight to
  `jest.mock`, e.g. `jest.mock('./Foo', () => () => null)`), assign
  **`.displayName`** on it before returning/exporting it, e.g.:

  ```ts
  jest.mock('./WSAudioPlayer', () => {
    const MockWSAudioPlayer = (props: WsAudioPlayerProps) => { ... };
    MockWSAudioPlayer.displayName = 'MockWSAudioPlayer';
    return { __esModule: true, default: MockWSAudioPlayer };
  });
  ```

- **Why:** satisfies `react/display-name` (anonymous components assigned to a
  `const` still trip the lint rule), and makes the component identifiable in
  React DevTools / test-failure component stacks instead of showing up as
  `Anonymous` or a generic mock name.
- Widely used across this repo's specs — see `MediaRecord.test.tsx`,
  `MediaPlayer.test.tsx`, `LimitedMediaPlayer.test.tsx`,
  `ConsultantCheck.test.tsx`, `ProfileDialog.test.tsx`,
  `ResourceOverview.test.tsx` — follow the `Mock<Name>` / `Mocked<Name>`
  naming convention already used there.

## Running a New Jest Test Module

Run Jest from `src/renderer` (required for this repo).

```bash
cd src/renderer
npm test -- <moduleName> --runInBand --watchAll=false
```
